{"reference":"1 Samuel 16","book":"1 Samuel","chapter":16,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Larger Story","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"This chapter marks the turn in First Samuel from Saul’s rejected kingship toward David’s story. The book moves from Eli’s failing priestly house through Samuel’s leadership and Saul’s kingship to David’s anointing, rise, exile, and Saul’s death. David’s anointing here therefore begins a major new movement in the book, even while Saul remains king.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_samuel_016_c62f5e94c4c4ee2f684a05f4"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Saul’s Troubling Spirit","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Verses 14–23 explicitly say that the Spirit of the LORD departed from Saul and that a harmful spirit from the LORD troubled him. The passage presents this in its own terms; it should not be reduced to a single medical, psychological, spiritual, or theological diagnosis. David’s harp playing brings Saul relief within the narrative, but the chapter does not explain the troubling experience beyond its stated attribution.","verse_refs":["1 Samuel 16:14-23"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_samuel_016_1047630360f3eb2acb95a1a6"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}